;; The agent-facing RULE SOURCE — the single authoritative statement of every rule an
;; agent is told to follow. It is rendered two ways by harness.rules:
;;
;;   - into the system prompt of each headless agent, filtered by :audience,
;;     with per-dispatch values substituted for {{placeholders}};
;;   - into the marker block of AGENTS.md by `bb rules-sync`, drift-checked by
;;     `bb rules-check` (which runs inside `bb gates`).
;;
;; The prompt rendering has a CLI of its own: `bb rules-prompt --audience coder`.
;;
;; Edit rules HERE — never in a prompt string, never by hand in AGENTS.md.
;; The gate exists because the same rule used to be written in two places with
;; nothing keeping them honest.
;;
;; WHY A RULE SOURCE AND NOT JUST A RULES FILE: a rules file reaches only clients
;; that read files. The method requires the agent that verifies the Coder to be
;; a DIFFERENT model family, and that agent reads no file at all — it gets an
;; HTTP request with a system prompt. Rendering into the prompt is the only
;; channel that reaches every family. It is also the only channel the project
;; controls absolutely: files merge with the reader's personal global rules,
;; which may contradict yours (see :precedence).
;;
;; AGENTS.md is the mirror because the Antigravity IDE, OpenCode and Pi read it
;; natively. Claude
;; Code reads only CLAUDE.md, so the seed ships a CLAUDE.md that IMPORTS AGENTS.md
;; rather than a second generated copy — one marker block, one drift target.
;;
;; Each rule:
;;   :id        stable keyword, referenced by tests
;;   :group     :non-negotiable | :conventions — the AGENTS.md section it lands in
;;   :audience  #{:coder :tester :human} — which renderings include it
;;   :title     bold lead-in for the AGENTS.md bullet
;;   :text      the rule as it reaches the agent; {{placeholders}} substituted
;;              per dispatch by harness.rules/render
;;   :from      provenance, when the rule is not originally yours
;;
;; LIMITATION: :text renders as ONE markdown bullet, so inline formatting (`code`,
;; *emphasis*) works and fenced code blocks, tables and sub-bullets do not. If a rule
;; needs those, extend harness.rules/markdown — do NOT hand-write it into AGENTS.md
;; outside the markers, which would reach only the clients that read that file and
;; silently skip the agent verifying the Coder.
;;
;; This is the KIT's rule source, and a project does NOT edit it: the clone it lives in is upgraded
;; with `git pull`. A project fills the three placeholder rules and adds rules of its own in its plan
;; repository's `rules.edn` - the OVERLAY `bb init` writes and `harness.rules/overlay` merges over this
;; file by :id; an entry for any other rule here is refused by name. Working ON the KIT, this file is
;; where a rule of the method is written.

[{:id :precedence
  :group :non-negotiable
  :audience #{:coder :tester :reviewer :human}
  :title "These rules win."
  :text "Where anything here conflicts with a personal, global, or editor-supplied instruction, THESE RULES are authoritative for this project, wherever you are reading them. Rule files merge silently and a global preference can contradict a project non-negotiable — if you notice a conflict, follow this rule and say so in your final message rather than picking one quietly."}

 {:id :repl-first
  :group :non-negotiable
  :audience #{:coder :tester :human}
  :title "REPL-first."
  :text "Prototype forms in the live nREPL BEFORE persisting files: {{eval-how}}. Always `:reload` when re-requiring a changed namespace."}

 {:id :repl-required
  :group :non-negotiable
  :audience #{:coder :tester :human}
  :from :awesome-copilot
  :title "No REPL, no edits."
  :text "If the nREPL is unreachable or evaluation stops working, STOP and say so in your final message. Never fall back to editing files blind — a dead REPL is a task failure the loop wants to see, not a reason to guess. The REPL is probed before you are dispatched, so this means it died mid-task."}

 {:id :no-gates
  :group :non-negotiable
  :audience #{:coder :tester :human}
  :title "Do not run the gates."
  :text "Do NOT run `bb gates`, `bb test`, or the coverage suite yourself. The loop runs every gate after you finish — that separation is load-bearing: authoring is your job, running is the gate's. Evaluating individual forms or a single test in the REPL while developing is fine and encouraged. The write tool repairs and lints each Clojure file as you write it and tells you what it found: acting on that is authoring, not running a gate, and the gates still run afterwards over the whole project."}

 {:id :packet-scope
  :group :non-negotiable
  :audience #{:coder :tester :human}
  :title "Stay inside your packet."
  :text "Write only your `:files/target`. `:files/context` files are read-only upstream dependencies — call only the signatures you were given in `:deps-sigs`, never modify them and never reach into their implementation. A check after assembly compares every call you make into a context namespace against `:deps-sigs`, and a call it did not grant fails the run before any gate; if you need one it lacks, say so with `note` rather than calling it."}

 {:id :review-read-only
  :group :non-negotiable
  :audience #{:reviewer :human}
  :title "Review produces findings, not edits."
  :text "You write nothing. You have a diff, a green gate report and read-only context — no `:files/target` and no REPL. An edge case you surface returns to the Tester as a new case and to the Coder for the fix; naming it is your job, fixing it is not."}

 {:id :review-scope
  :group :non-negotiable
  :audience #{:reviewer :human}
  :title "Judge only what the gates cannot."
  :text "Every gate is already green before you are dispatched — formatting, lint, independently-authored tests and layer boundaries have all passed. Re-checking them spends the one judgement step the loop pays for on work a free check already did. Judge what no tool can: design, idiom, logic, naming, edge cases and silent behaviour changes. A dependency on a concrete implementation where a protocol crosses the seam is a finding; so is a workaround that hides an infrastructure fault instead of failing fast."}

 {:id :layer-boundaries
  :group :non-negotiable
  :audience #{:coder :human}
  :title "Layer boundaries are enforced by a gate."
  :text "Respect the boundaries of layer {{layer}}. Depend on PROTOCOLS across a seam, never on a concrete implementation. <Name your layers and their allowed direction here; the composition root is exempt.>"}

 {:id :root-cause
  :group :non-negotiable
  :audience #{:coder :tester :human}
  :from :awesome-copilot
  :title "Fix the cause, not the symptom."
  :text "Never add a workaround or a fallback for an infrastructure problem — a missing service, a broken REPL, an absent file. Fail fast and clearly instead, so the loop surfaces the real fault."}

 {:id :note-the-contract
  :group :non-negotiable
  :audience #{:coder :tester :reviewer}
  :title "Say what the contract did not."
  :text "When the Blueprint is silent about a case you had to decide, when a `:deps-sigs` entry looks wrong, or when you made an assumption someone should check — call the `note` tool. Do NOT save it for your final message: a note is carried into the next dispatch as feedback and a final message is not, so an observation left only in prose dies with your turn. Deciding quietly is the failure this prevents; the next role derives from the same contract and will decide differently."}

 {:id :label-fabricated
  :group :non-negotiable
  :audience #{:coder :tester :reviewer :human}
  :title "Label anything you made up, where it is shown."
  :text "Mark fabricated, mocked, stubbed or example values AT THE POINT THEY ARE DISPLAYED — in the row, the cell, the return value — never only in a caption or a sentence nearby. A plausible number in a real-looking frame is read as a measurement whatever the surrounding prose says, so prefer values that cannot be mistaken for real. Never present a hand-written value as something a tool produced, and when you report that something was verified, say which part actually executed."}

 {:id :align-forms
  :group :conventions
  :audience #{:coder :tester :human}
  :from :awesome-copilot
  :title "Indent the way cljfmt does."
  :text "Indent a multi-line form the way cljfmt does: successive elements of a data structure or arg vector sit under the first, one per line. A form whose elements drift out of alignment makes a bracket balancer close brackets in the WRONG place, and gate 0 then rewrites your code into something you did not write. Never pad with extra spaces to line values into columns — cljfmt does not align `cond` or `let` values, and `:else    x` FAILS the fmt gate."}

 {:id :inline-def
  :group :conventions
  :audience #{:coder :tester :human}
  :from :awesome-copilot
  :title "Inline `def` over `println`."
  :text "Debug by `def`-ing the intermediate value and evaluating it, not by printing it: the binding stays inspectable for the next form, and the return value is what the loop captures."}

 {:id :report-evaluations
  :group :conventions
  :audience #{:coder :tester}
  :from :awesome-copilot
  :title "Report what you evaluated."
  :text "Nobody is watching your REPL session. Your final message is the only trace the Reviewer and triage will ever see, so state what you evaluated and what came back — not merely that you are done."}

 {:id :review-report
  :group :conventions
  :audience #{:reviewer}
  :title "Your final message is the whole review."
  :text "Nobody reads your reasoning — only your output. Name each finding with the file and the form it concerns, and say explicitly when you found nothing. An empty review and a skipped review are otherwise indistinguishable to triage."}

 {:id :no-shadowing
  :group :conventions
  :audience #{:coder :tester :human}
  :from :awesome-copilot
  :title "Do not shadow clojure.core."
  :text "Never bind or def over core names — `map`, `filter`, `count`, `name`, `type`, `key`, `val`, `get`, `set`, and friends."}

 {:id :test-no-docstring
  :group :conventions
  :audience #{:tester :human}
  :title "`deftest` / `defspec` take no docstring."
  :text "A bare string in the body is an \"unused value\" warning, and the lint gate fails on WARNINGS, not just errors. Put the intent in the test name or a ;; comment above the form."}

 {:id :no-toplevel-side-effects
  :group :conventions
  :audience #{:tester :human}
  :title "No top-level side effects in test namespaces."
  :text "No `(run-tests)` at the top level — that is a REPL-ism, and it fails the gates."}

 {:id :generators-are-values
  :group :conventions
  :audience #{:tester :human}
  :title "Generators and properties are values."
  :text "Bind them with `def`, not `defn`. And a generator is a VALUE, not a function to call: `gen/large-integer`, never `(gen/large-integer)` — the call fails when the test file loads, after the author has gone. `defspec` shape: `(defspec name num-tests (prop/for-all [...] ...))`."}

 ;; "— once" is measured. Runs D7 (fold), D8 (bind) and D10 (measure) each had a
 ;; Coder validate at every recursive call, re-checking every subtree: quadratic
 ;; on deep input. D10's measure took 3,780ms at 800 nodes and 10ms once
 ;; validation moved to the entry. The Coder had "validate at the seams" and a
 ;; docstring saying "Seams throw; interiors assume" open, and did it anyway, three
 ;; times, so the fix is the specificity those lacked, not a second copy (method
 ;; §10 lesson 12). NOTES.md row 12.
 {:id :shapes-are-the-contract
  :group :conventions
  ;; THE REVIEWER IS TOLD THIS TOO (2026-09-15). It reached the Coder alone, so no Reviewer's
  ;; prompt named the defect two early runs had carried — validation on every recursive call — and
  ;; Reviewers sampled on those two cases found it seldom. A bake-off re-ran both cases with this
  ;; rule in the Reviewer's prompt; its records are not in this repository.
  :audience #{:coder :human :reviewer}
  :title "Shapes are the contract."
  :text "Data-first: agree the shapes before the functions, and validate at the seams — once. A seam is a public entry, not every call: a function that recurses into itself crosses its own seam on every call and re-validates every subtree, which is quadratic on deep input. Validate at the entry, then recurse through a private helper that assumes the shape. <Name your validation library and the ex-info type a boundary failure throws.>"}

 ;; THE THIRD PLACEHOLDER, and the one two projects needed most. The first had three stops in a row
 ;; from one cause: its loader established things about the set of pages it returned - one home page,
 ;; distinct slugs, validated there and nowhere else - and every role past it received a bare vector,
 ;; read the shapes, found nothing forbidding the bad input, and was right to object. The second
 ;; drew spec-review findings on every spec, many of them one question no single spec
 ;; could answer: what a tree is in this project and how it is walked. Written once here it reached
 ;; every role and the spec review; first-review findings fell, and two
 ;; definitions added before the next stage (what "the text of" an element is; what "carries an
 ;; attribute" means) were never asked about again.
 ;;
 ;; EVERY AUDIENCE, THE TESTER INCLUDED. `:shapes-are-the-contract` reaches the Coder and the
 ;; Reviewer; a guarantee written into its placeholder does not reach the role most likely to test
 ;; an input the guarantee rules out.
 {:id :data-conventions
  :group :conventions
  :audience #{:coder :tester :reviewer :human}
  :title "This project's data conventions."
  :text "<Say once, for every role, what this project's recurring data IS. What a tree or a document is made of, and how it is walked (through what, never into what). What collection type each recurring argument is, and in what order. What your targets' recurring words mean - \"the text of\", \"carries\", \"appears in\". What a boundary failure looks like to a caller - that to refuse is to throw, say, and with what. THE THREE THINGS A TESTER OTHERWISE LEARNS ONE TRY AT A TIME: that a shape var is data and not a function to call; what a fixture is - what the seam before the function under test would produce, named in the target that needs it; and that a test file given to it exists to be edited, not rewritten. WHERE INPUT COMES FROM - a file format, a request, another namespace - because a value that source cannot produce is outside every domain, and no test or review finding is about one. And what each seam GUARANTEES to everything past it: the properties downstream code assumes and must not re-check, and that no test or review finding may ask it to handle. A question two specs would both have to answer belongs here and in neither spec. AND WHETHER THE TEMPLATE'S OWN TEST SUITE IS THE PATTERN: a generated application may ship a test that boots the system inside the test gate on purpose - say so, name it as the one exception, and keep the rule for what a Tester writes (nothing a Tester writes starts a server).>"}

 {:id :types-as-the-language-defines-them
  :group :conventions
  ;; Set by a person, after every reader of one contract - two roles, a blind judge, a review
  ;; nine times over - called a vector's order "unstated" and acted on it. It was not unstated;
  ;; the language states it. The Coder that sorted the vector had changed the contract; the
  ;; Tester that asserted the constructed order was right; the route was the Coder's. Tested:
  ;; the first sentence alone changed nothing; with the corollary the false finding fell from
  ;; nine of nine to three of nine, and what remained was true. GENERAL ON PURPOSE - no type is
  ;; named. Vector and set were the instance, not the rule.
  :audience #{:coder :tester :reviewer :human}
  :title "Types are followed as the language defines them."
  :text "A type named in a contract means exactly what the language specification says it means - no more, no less - and that meaning is part of the contract without being written out. Do not add to it (a role that reorders, de-duplicates, coerces or otherwise departs from what the type defines has changed the contract) and do not call it unstated (a finding that asks for a sentence the type already provides is not a finding). In doubt, the language specification is the referee, not a reader's sense of what was meant. An ordered collection built from ordered inputs has the order of its construction: that order is given, not left open, and a role that imposes another has changed the contract. The type decides what a value IS, not that every value of the type can ARRIVE: where this project's data conventions name where input comes from, a value that source cannot produce is outside the domain. Where they name no source, every value the type admits is in it."}

 {:id :formatting
  :group :conventions
  :audience #{:coder :tester :human}
  :title "Formatting is enforced by a gate."
  :text "Run no formatter yourself — write clean and let gate 0 and the fmt gate handle the drift."}]
